   116	1 INTRODUCTION
   117	
   118	
   119	Sound and precise call graphs are a prerequisite for inter-procedural
   120	static analysis. Over the past decades, dozens of call-graph algorithms for object-oriented programming languages have been proposed [ 1, 4, 9, 24, 29 ]. However, their implementations have inconsistent support for crucial language features, e.g., reflection, serialization, or threads—they often support these features unsoundly,
   121	or not at all [16, 17, 26].
   122	
   123	Table 1 shows the state of affairs for the WALA [ 11 ] and Soot [ 30 ]
   124	frameworks. We generated the table by reproducing Reif et al.’s [ 16 ]
   125	study of soundness [1] of JVM-language call-graph algorithms. We
   126	used the current version of their benchmark [2] —a suite of manu
   127	ally annotated tests—and current versions of WALA (1.5.7) and
   128	Soot (4.4.1) for the call-graph algorithms Class-Hierarchy Analysis
   129	(CHA) [ 4 ], Rapid-Type Analysis (RTA) [ 1 ], Control-Flow Analysis
   130	(CFA) [24], and Soot’s default configuration of SPARK [13].
   131	While the exact numbers have changed, the overall picture stays
   132	the same as reported by Reif et al. [ 16 ]: Language feature support
   133	varies significantly not only across frameworks but even across
   134	algorithms within the same framework. In general, more precise
   135	call-graph algorithms become less sound. All call-graph algorithms
   136	of WALA and Soot fail to soundly analyze about half of the test
   137	cases. For some features, call-graph algorithms even fail all test
   138	
   139	
   140	1 None of these call-graph algorithms are sound in the mathematical sense. In this
   141	paper, we refer to the term soundness as a lower number of missing edges in call graphs.
   142	This is in line with related work [16, 26].
