   190	In some cases, less precise call-graph algorithms like CHA and RTA
   191	pass tests due to excessive imprecision rather than to actual feature
   192	support. Surprisingly, WALA’s CHA is not only less precise but
   193	apparently also less sound than WALA’s RTA. The inconsistent
   194	support of language features makes it difficult for users to systematically choose an appropriate call-graph algorithm. One has to
   195	know in detail which language features each algorithm supports
   196	soundly and cannot decide based on precision and performance
   197	alone. This also makes it difficult for researchers to compare callgraph algorithms and implementations in terms of precision and
   198	performance.
   199	In this paper, we analyze reasons for this observed inconsistency
   200	and propose a solution to the problem. In short, the problem is that
   201	different call-graph algorithms handle language features in specific
   202	ways by making specific use of different kinds of information they
   203	have access to. For example, a CFA algorithm can soundly handle
   204	more reflection calls because it has access to pointer information.
   205	CHA and RTA handle reflection differently because they do not
   206	have access to pointer information. As a result, code that handles
   207	call resolution for individual language features is coupled to specific call-graph algorithms, which makes it difficult to reuse that
   208	code across different call-graph algorithms. Developers wanting to
   209	support new language features have to duplicate code to support
   210	dissimilar algorithms. Thus, the available resources and priorities
   211	of developers of a certain framework determine which features are
   212	supported by which algorithm; maintenance is also complicated as
   213	features evolve.
   214	
   215	The work presented in this paper demonstrates that it is possible
   216	to implement a variety of call-graph algorithms with consistent
   217	handling of language features. Specifically, we propose Unimocg
   218	(UNIfied MOdular Call Graphs), a novel architecture for modular
   219	implementation of call-graph algorithms that decouples the implementation of the following concerns: (1) computation of type
   220	information, (2) interpretation of type information, (3) resolution
   221	
   222	
   223	|Call Resolver|Col2|
   224	|---|---|
   225	|Virtual Calls|Reﬂection|
   226	|Serialization|Threads|
   227	
   228	
   229	
   230	Arrows indicate dependencies between components
   231	
   232	
   233	Figure 1: Unimocg’s modular call-graph architecture
   234	
   235	
   236	of calls, and (4) analyses that depend on type information. Fig. 1
   237	overviews the components that handle these concerns in Unimocg
   238	and their relations.
   239	
   240	Type producers compute information about the runtime types of
   241	variables and fields. Type iterators interpret this type information
   242	and make it available to call resolvers and type consumers—keeping
   243	them decoupled from type producers. Call resolvers resolve method
   244	calls or calls of language features such as reflection, serialization, or
   245	threads. They query a type iterator for type information about call
   246	receivers or arguments of reflective calls; in turn, the information
   247	about resolved method calls is used by type producers. Type consumers are static analyses that depend on type information but do
   248	not contribute to call-graph construction. Without type iterators,
   249	type consumers would have to rely on imprecise type information,
   250	e.g., provided by static types, to avoid dependence on a specific
   251	call-graph algorithm. For instance, the immutability analysis by
   252	Roth et al. [ 20 ] uses imprecise type information from static types
   253	and the class hierarchy.
   254	Unimocg’s modular architecture enables deriving call graphs
   255	with consistent coverage of language features and hence soundness
   256	by reusing and combining type producers, type iterator, and call
   257	resolvers in a plug-n-play manner. We show that Unimocg enables
   258	a wide range of call-graph algorithms to share the same support
   259	for language features such as reflection and serialization, thus ensuring consistent soundness, by implementing ten algorithms from
   260	different families: CHA [ 4 ], RTA [ 1 ], the XTA family with MTA,
   261	FTA, and CTA [ 29 ], as well as 푘 - 푙 -CFA-based algorithms 0-CFA,
   262	0-1-CFA, 1-0-CFA, and 1-1-CFA [9].
   263	Type consumers also benefit from the modular architecture by
   264	reusing precise type information computed for call-graph construction. To showcase this, we reimplemented the immutability analysis
   265	by Roth et al. [ 20 ] as a type consumer in Unimocg. Unimocg’s separation of call-graph construction from the computation of type
   266	information ensures consistent and improved precision of the immutability analysis when combined with more precise call graphs.
   267	Unimocg benefits both users and developers of static analyses
   268	(call-graph and other analyses). Users can rely on consistent soundness and can systematically choose appropriate algorithms for their
