   987	4.3 Impact on Precision and Performance
   988	
   989	
   990	Looking only at test cases passed could lead to incorrect conclusions if test cases were passed only due to excessive imprecision
   991	or come at a significant performance cost. Thus, we show that
   992	Unimocg’s consistent soundness does not compromise precision
   993	or performance (RQ3). To this end, we compare three different
   994	call-graph algorithms: CHA, RTA, and 0-CFA. These algorithms
   995	are available in all frameworks being compared, Unimocg, Soot,
   996	and WALA (0-CFA only for WALA and Unimocg). We run the subject algorithms on five Java applications from XCorpus [ 5 ], which
   997	were also used by Reif et al. [ 16 ]. We used the Adoptium OpenJDK 1.8.0_342-b07 that worked with all frameworks. The different
   998	frameworks by default exclude different parts of the JDK from the
   999	analysis, thus, we configured all frameworks to analyze the full JDK
  1000	for comparability. Soot’s RTA, however, can not analyze the full
  1001	JDK 8 as that contains MethodHandle constants that Soot’s RTA is
  1002	not prepared to handle (Soot’s SPARK has the same problem). We
  1003	thus ran Soot RTA with its default configuration excluding large
  1004	parts of the JDK. The results are shown in Table 3.
  1005	Precision. Following common methodology [ 25, 29 ], we measure
  1006	precision by counting the number of reachable methods—more
  1007	precise call graphs have fewer reachable methods.
  1008	Unimocg’s call-graph algorithms do not suffer precision degradation compared to Soot’s and WALA’s. Unimocg’s CHA is comparable to both Soot’s and WALA’s ( ∼ 5% and ∼ 18% more methods,
  1009	respectively). Soot’s RTA, even though excluding large parts of
  1010	the JDK, has on average 1.7x and WALA’s has 7x the reachable
  1011	
  1012	
  1013	
  1014	methods of Unimocg’s RTA. WALA’s CFA has on average 2x the
  1015	reachable methods of Unimocg’s. While precision across different frameworks differs significantly and is difficult to compare
  1016	precisely, the numbers clearly indicate that Unimocg’s algorithms
  1017	do not suffer from systematic imprecision, on the contrary. This
  1018	is explainable: modularity helps not only to cover more features
  1019	orthogonally, increasing soundness; it also makes it easier to implement more precise call resolution because call resolvers make
  1020	use of the precise type information provided by type producers.
  1021	Also note that for Unimocg, the number of reachable methods
  1022	decreases significantly when we use more precise type producers; e.g., its RTA identifies around 90% fewer reachable methods
  1023	than its CHA. This matches the expectations of call-graph users and
  1024	shows that reusing the same call-resolver modules across call-graph
  1025	algorithms does not impair their relative precision. Specifically, individual call resolvers use the type information gathered by the
  1026	chosen type producer and thus work at a consistent level of precision. This is not possible if the computation of type information is
  1027	tightly coupled to the resolution of virtual calls; in this case, modules for other language features would rely on a fixed, potentially
  1028	ad-hoc, method of computing type information.
  1029	Performance. Table 3 reports analysis runtime in seconds as median of 3 executions on a server with two AMD(R) EPYC(R) 7542
  1030	@ 2.90 GHz (32 cores / 64 threads each) CPUs and 512 GB RAM.
  1031	Unimocg’s call-graph algorithms do not suffer performance
  1032	degradation, either. The most notable finding is the difference in
  1033	RTA performance. Unimocg’s RTA is on average 14x and 35x faster
  1034	than RTA of Soot (again, excluding large parts of the JDK), resp.
  1035	WALA. This is noteworthy because to enable reusing call-graph
  1036	modules, e.g., for the one resolving reflection, Soot and WALA emulate RTA by a points-to analysis. Our evaluation indicates that
  1037	this approach seems to come at a significant performance cost. Unimocg’s 0-CFA is 2.4x slower than WALA’s 0-CFA across applications
  1038	in the benchmark except jext, on which WALA’s 0-CFA took over
  1039	36 minutes and Unimocg only took 86 seconds. Unimocg’s CHA is
  1040	5x slower than WALA’s, but 6x faster than Soot’s. We attribute this
  1041	to OPAL’s intermediate representation, which employs abstract
  1042	interpretation [ 18 ] to provide refined type information but needs
  1043	more computation time per reachable method; Soot’s Jimple [ 31 ],
  1044	while not based on abstract interpretation, offers similar information. Note that performance differences between frameworks may
  1045	be due to implementation details beyond our proposed architecture.
  1046	Thus, this experiment cannot be used to draw conclusions about absolute performance, only show that Unimocg does not compromise
  1047	performance because of its modularity.
